  - TITLE: COMMENT
    TYPE: String
    REQUIRED: False
  - TITLE: RELATIONS
    TYPE: String
    REQUIRED: False

[SECTION]
TITLE: Reliability

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-DIR-001
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: No exceptions cross the String_Types filesystem boundary
STATEMENT: >>>
None of the five filesystem functions (``Exists``, ``Kind``,
``Create_Directory``, ``Delete_File``, ``Rename``) SHALL propagate any
exception across the ``String_Types`` public boundary.

All exceptions, including ``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Name_Error``,
``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Use_Error``, ``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Status_Error``,
``Constraint_Error``, and, to the maximum extent permitted by the Ada runtime,
``Storage_Error``, SHALL be absorbed by the implementation and translated into
the designated safe return values:

- ``Boolean``-returning functions return ``False`` on any exception.
- ``Kind`` returns ``Special_File`` on any exception.

This totality requirement applies identically across all five backends.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
``Storage_Error`` is raised by the Ada runtime for memory exhaustion or stack
overflow conditions.  Ada RM 11.6 explicitly allows handlers for
``Storage_Error`` but advises caution; absorbing it here is a best-effort
commitment.  If a backend's Ada runtime cannot safely handle ``Storage_Error``
in an exception handler, it MAY re-propagate it; this exception is therefore
listed as "maximum extent permitted" rather than mandatory.

The primary motivation is the Aclida CLI ``OS.Files`` shim, which currently
contains per-call exception handlers to work around the absence of this
contract in the Ada standard library.  Centralising the absorption in the
bridge eliminates that boilerplate from every consumer.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-002, FUNC-DIR-003, FUNC-DIR-004, FUNC-DIR-005, FUNC-DIR-006

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Dependencies

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-DIR-002
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: No new external dependencies introduced
STATEMENT: >>>
The filesystem boundary feature SHALL use only:

- Standard Ada library packages (``Ada.Directories``,
  ``Ada.IO_Exceptions``, ``Ada.Strings.UTF_Encoding``,
  ``Ada.Characters.*``).
- The per-backend encoding helpers already present in each backend's
  ``*_operations`` packages at the time this feature is implemented.

No new entries SHALL be added to ``alire.toml`` dependency declarations as a
direct result of implementing this feature.

The ``uxstring`` and ``vss`` backends MAY use the UTF-8 serialisation
primitives already provided by their respective external crates (which are
already declared in ``alire.toml``); those are not new dependencies.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This preserves the library's no-runtime-dependency contract for the default
(``standard``) build target.  A consumer who builds with the ``standard``
backend and does not need ``uxstrings`` or ``vss`` SHALL NOT have those crates
pulled into their dependency graph as a result of this feature.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-007

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: SPARK Compatibility

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-DIR-003
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: SPARK_Mode Off scoped to the filesystem subsection of anystrings.ads
STATEMENT: >>>
The filesystem-related declarations added to ``src/anystrings.ads``
(the generic signature package) SHALL be wrapped in a ``pragma SPARK_Mode (Off)``
region.  The region SHALL encompass only the ``File_Kind`` enumeration
(FUNC-DIR-001) and the five filesystem formal subprograms
(FUNC-DIR-002 through FUNC-DIR-006).

The ``pragma SPARK_Mode (Off)`` SHALL NOT extend beyond the filesystem
subsection.  All pre-existing declarations in ``src/anystrings.ads`` that are
currently under ``SPARK_Mode On`` SHALL remain under ``SPARK_Mode On``.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
``Ada.Directories`` is not covered by the SPARK subset (Ada RM A.16 calls are
not SPARK-analyzable by GNATprove).  Wrapping only the new subsection in
``SPARK_Mode (Off)`` follows the existing pattern in ``src/anystrings.ads``
where I/O and environment operations are individually guarded rather than
disabling SPARK_Mode for the whole package.

The ``File_Kind`` enumeration itself has no SPARK-incompatible features, but
it is placed inside the ``SPARK_Mode (Off)`` region because it is semantically
coupled to the filesystem operations and GNATprove would need to follow the
enumeration to analyze the formal subprograms that reference it.
<<<
RELATIONS: NFR-CONV-001

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Platform Scope

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-DIR-004
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Specified behaviour applies to POSIX filesystems only
STATEMENT: >>>
The functional requirements FUNC-DIR-002 through FUNC-DIR-007 specify
behaviour for POSIX-compliant filesystems (Linux and macOS).

Behaviour on Windows filesystems is explicitly out of scope.  On Windows,
the UTF-8 path encoding specified in FUNC-DIR-007 and the totality semantics
of NFR-DIR-001 MAY not hold due to Windows-specific path encoding conventions
(UTF-16 wide paths, drive letters, UNC paths) and Win32 error code mappings
that the Ada ``Ada.Directories`` binding may not fully abstract.

Implementations SHOULD NOT add Windows-specific code paths; undefined
behaviour on Windows is preferable to a silently incorrect implementation.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The primary consumers of this library (Aclida CLI and related tooling) target
POSIX platforms.  Windows support would require at minimum a separate encoding
path (UTF-16 via ``Ada.Wide_Directories`` or platform-specific bindings) and
a separate set of error-mapping tests; both are out of scope for this feature
increment.

This requirement is recorded explicitly so that future contributors who
attempt a Windows port know the exact gap to fill and do not mistake the
current implementation for a working Windows implementation.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-007

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Backward Compatibility

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-DIR-005
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Additive change — no existing String_Types public name modified or removed
STATEMENT: >>>
The filesystem boundary feature SHALL be a purely additive change to the
``String_Types`` public API.

No existing public name (function, procedure, type, constant, or subtype)
exposed by any of the five backends' ``string_types.ads`` files or by
``src/anystrings.ads`` SHALL be removed, renamed, or have its signature
changed as a result of implementing this feature.

A consumer of any current ``String_Types`` backend whose source code does not
reference the new filesystem API SHALL recompile without modification after
the feature is implemented.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This mirrors the additive-change constraint established for the
conversion-semantics feature (NFR-CONV-003) and the command-line access
feature.  It protects existing downstream consumers (including the test suite
and the examples crate) from inadvertent breakage during the implementation
phase.
<<<
RELATIONS: NFR-CONV-003, FUNC-DIR-008

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Quality

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: NFR-DIR-006
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Test coverage for filesystem operations
STATEMENT: >>>
The project's test suite SHALL include AUnit test cases that verify the
filesystem boundary feature across all five backends.  The test suite SHALL
cover at minimum:

1. ``Exists`` returns ``False`` for a non-existent path (totality check for
   query functions).
2. ``Kind`` returns ``Special_File`` for a non-existent path (totality check
   for the kind query).
3. ``Create_Directory``, ``Exists``, ``Kind``, and ``Delete_File`` on a path
   composed entirely of ASCII characters — the create/existence/kind/delete
   round-trip.
4. The same round-trip on a path containing at least one UTF-8 multibyte
   character (e.g. a directory named ``café`` or ``日本語``) to exercise the
   encoding path of FUNC-DIR-007.
5. ``Rename`` on an ASCII path — source exists before rename, source does not
   exist and destination does exist after rename.
6. ``Rename`` returns ``False`` when the source path does not exist.

Tests SHOULD be structured as a single AUnit fixture file that compiles
against any backend, consistent with the pattern established by NFR-CONV-006
for the conversion-semantics tests.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The UTF-8 multibyte path test (item 4) is the regression test for the
byte-corruption bug described in FUNC-DIR-007: if the implementation
incorrectly uses ``To_Latin_1`` for path encoding on a Unicode backend, the
test will fail because the directory will not be found under the correctly-
encoded name.

The ``standard`` backend's UTF-8 multibyte path test exercises the
byte-passthrough path.  If the host uses a non-UTF-8 locale, the test is
permitted to skip with a recorded note rather than fail.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-002, FUNC-DIR-003, FUNC-DIR-004, FUNC-DIR-005, FUNC-DIR-006, FUNC-DIR-007, NFR-CONV-006

[/SECTION]
